# Booster Rocket's environment. Copy this to .env for local development (a server run with docker compose uses
# .env.poc, which scripts/poc/setup.sh makes from .env.poc.example). Never commit the copy: it holds secrets.
#
# These are the only values set outside the portal. Everything else (the club's name and color, staff sign-in, outgoing
# mail, QuickBooks, parent sign-ins) is set up in the portal itself, at /setup the first time and under Settings after.

# Where the portal's Postgres database is: postgres://user:password@host:port/database
DATABASE_URL=postgres://booster:booster@localhost:5433/booster

# The portal's own address, the one people type into a browser: https://portal.yourclub.org
# Only the address: http or https, the name, a port if it has one. No path and nothing after it.
# Sign-in, the links in emails, the address QuickBooks returns to and the installed app are all built on it, so it must
# be the address people really use. The setup page and the staff Health page show it, and warn when a browser reached
# the portal under a different one. If it changes, update the redirect addresses at Microsoft, Google and Intuit too.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Signs the session cookies of both doors (staff and parents), and the worker's token is made from it.
# 32 or more random characters: openssl rand -hex 32
# Changing it signs everyone out. Keep it secret, and keep it with your backups.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=change-me-to-32-or-more-random-characters

# Encrypts the secrets the portal stores in its database: the QuickBooks connection, the staff sign-in certificate and
# client secret, and the mail password. 32 random bytes, base64: openssl rand -base64 32
# Back it up with the database. Without it a restored database can't be read where it matters: QuickBooks, staff sign-in
# and mail would each have to be set up again.
SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Only while changing SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY: the key before the change, so what was stored under it can still be read.
# Leave it out otherwise.
# SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Which request header says where a visitor is coming from. The portal uses the address for the sign-in limits and
# writes it in the audit log. It is the header that the proxy in front of the portal sets itself, replacing whatever
# the visitor sent. Any other header can be typed by a visitor, to dodge the limits or to put a false address in the log,
# so the portal reads this one header and no other. When the header holds a list, the last address in it is used: the
# one your proxy added.
#   cf-connecting-ip   behind Cloudflare, tunnel or proxy (this is what is used when the line is left out)
#   x-forwarded-for    behind nginx, Caddy, Traefik, an AWS or Azure load balancer, Azure App Service
#   x-real-ip          behind nginx set up with: proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
#   fly-client-ip      on Fly.io
#   none               no proxy you control is in front of the portal: every address is recorded as unknown, and
#                      the sign-in limits per address are not applied (only those per email and per code are)
# With more than one proxy in front, name the header the outermost one sets and the others pass on untouched.
# Choose a header the proxy doesn't set and every address is unknown, as with none; the Health page says so once
# parents have signed in. Choose one a visitor can send and a visitor can pretend to be at any address, and get past
# the limits per address. Find out what your host's proxy sets rather than guessing: none costs the audit log every
# address and the sign-in door its flood limit.
# CLIENT_IP_HEADER=cf-connecting-ip
